      shortDescription: 'A differential mechanism that prevents all engine power from spinning a single slipping wheel, transferring torque to the wheel with grip.',
      fullDescription: 'A standard open differential sends power along the path of least resistance — meaning if one tire slips on ice, gravel, or under hard cornering, it spins uselessly while the gripping tire gets zero torque. An LSD limits this wheel-speed difference, locking or biasing torque to ensure the tire with traction accelerates the car.',
      howItWorks: ['Internal clutch packs, helical gears, or viscous fluid sense wheel speed differentials.','When one wheel loses traction, internal friction locks the axle halves partially together.','Up to 80% of torque transfers across to the wheel with maximum grip.','Vehicle accelerates crisply out of corners without inside-wheel wheelspin.'],
